From 55a5996343f5c77b36725b79ac4ba5b4a3d04dc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:46:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Add a riscv/esp32c3db port

Include the final pbuf pool and heap sizing in the initial port.
The original descriptions below retain their historical measurements
and validation limits; the final configuration is eight 1520-byte
pbufs and an 8 KiB heap, as supplied by the last sizing fix.
The last fix has no author/date headers; those are inferred from
AtomVM commit 57de16db46a63b82c7880199ddc875870338b680.

Original-patch: 0001-add-riscv-esp32c3db-port.patch
Original-author: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Original-date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:46:11 -0400

Add a riscv/esp32c3db port

lwIP now builds and runs on the ESP32-C3.  A test application reaches the
tcpip thread and gets a descriptor back from socket():

  *** ESP32C3 LWIP TEST ***
  esp32c3: the stack is up but there is no interface. ...
  start_networking returned 1 (1 expected: no driver linked)
  socket() = 3

Three files, and two of them are shaped by things this part does not have.

The sizing is not tuning.  Every default in rtemslwip/include/lwipopts.h is
#ifndef-guarded and they were chosen for parts with external DRAM: MEM_SIZE is
2 MiB and the pbuf pool is 512 buffers of 1600 bytes, 800 KiB.  That is 2848
KiB against a 320 KiB RAM region -- it cannot link, let alone run, and the
ESP32-C3 has no external RAM interface to fix it with, SOC_SPIRAM_SUPPORTED
being undefined for this part.  The values here total 70 KiB of the 218 KiB
free once RTEMS and the WiFi libraries have taken theirs, and the linked image
shows them: ram_heap 0x800c, memp_memory_PBUF_POOL_base 0x9780.

TCP_SND_QUEUELEN has to be set alongside TCP_SND_BUF rather than left alone.
lwIP derives it as 16 * TCP_SND_BUF / TCP_MSS, which came to 64 segments
against MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG of 24, and lwIP's own sanity check in core/init.c
refuses to build rather than let the queue outrun the pool.  It is right to:
the failure would otherwise be a transmit stall under load rather than an
error.  That check caught a real inconsistency in the first version of this
file.

netstart.c is unlike every other BSP's because the ESP32-C3 has no Ethernet
controller.  Its only interface is the WiFi radio, driven by Espressif's binary
libraries -- eight closed archives and a 120-entry OS shim, which are not part
of rtems-lwip and should not become part of it.  So start_networking() brings
the stack up and leaves the interface to a weak esp32c3_netif_add() that the
WiFi driver overrides.  The dependency then points the right way: rtems-lwip
knows nothing about the WiFi port and the WiFi port knows about lwIP.  An
application that links no driver still gets loopback, the socket API and the
tcpip thread, plus a message saying why it has no interface -- rather than a
link error naming a symbol it has never heard of.

LWIP_NETIF_STATUS_CALLBACK is on, which rtems-lwip leaves off by default.
esp_wifi_internal_set_sta_ip() needs it to tell the WiFi libraries the
station's address so they can answer ARP while the CPU sleeps, and on this part
the only interface is the one that wants it.

Not proposed upstream: this is on a branch of the fork, and the numbers should
be revisited against measured traffic rather than derived from the part, as
they are here.

Signed-off-by: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

Original-patch: 0002-esp32c3-tune-pbuf-pool-and-heap-size.patch
Original-author: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Original-date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 23:58:30 -0400

esp32c3: Halve the pbuf pool and the lwIP heap

The ESP32-C3 has no PSRAM, so the whole writable world is 272 KiB of SRAM,
and lwIP was holding 71.5 KiB of it in two static arrays where ESP-IDF
allocates from the heap.  That is most of the reason this port spends 88.5 KiB
more static SRAM than a stock ESP-IDF build and has 85 KiB less heap, and the
margin it leaves is what makes the radio fail: with 2128 bytes free the WiFi
libraries answer ESP_ERR_NO_MEM, which surfaces as a scan that finds zero
networks rather than as an allocation error.

  PBUF_POOL_SIZE     24 -> 12     one TCP_WND is 4 segments, and the tcpip
                                  mailbox backlog is small; 12 is about three
                                  times what a single station needs
  PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE  1600 -> 1520 RTEMS_ESP_NETIF_FRAME_MAX is 1518 and the
                                  MTU is 1500; 1600 was the generic default
  MEM_SIZE           32K -> 16K   the only large tenant is one TCP_SND_BUF of
                                  unacked pbufs, plus one packet each for
                                  DHCP, DNS and mDNS

MEM_LIBC_MALLOC was considered and rejected.  It would remove ram_heap
entirely, but the thing that runs out of the *other* pool reports an empty
scan, and merging them turns an lwIP burst into a radio that looks dead.  The
wall between the two is what makes "which heap ran out" answerable.

Measured, not predicted: memp_memory_PBUF_POOL_base 38784 -> 18432 and
ram_heap 32780 -> 16396, and the work area of examples/wifi-net grows 135248
-> 171984.  36736 bytes, exactly the arithmetic.

WHAT IS AND IS NOT PROVEN

The saving is proven.  The sizes are not proven under load, and that needs
saying because a pool that is too small does not fail as an error -- it fails
as a radio that goes quiet.

examples/wifi-net now prints lwip_stats for the pool and the heap and asserts
that neither ever refused an allocation.  That check is shown to have teeth:
holding all 12 entries gives "pbuf pool 12 of 12 used, max 12, refused 136",
rx_dropped_no_pbuf 136, and the assertion FAILs.

But the run that is supposed to establish the size could not be performed
here.  It needs a host that can flood the station, and this access point does
not pass client-to-client traffic: 1.88 million sendto() calls to the
station's own leased address returned EHOSTUNREACH while it held that lease.
Against background traffic alone -- 1917 frames over 40 seconds -- the pool
never needed more than one entry, so holding 10 of 12 also passed.  A soak on
a network that allows it is still owed.

Signed-off-by: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

Original-patch: 0006-esp32c3-leave-heap-for-network-driver.patch
Original-author: Peter M <petermm@gmail.com>
Original-date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 08:14:52 +0200

Leave C3 heap space for the public network driver

Use the C5/S31 lwIP heap and packet pool counts so scanning and WPA can
coexist with the network gen_server on C3.

Co-authored-by: Peter M <petermm@gmail.com>
---
 defs/bsps/riscv/esp32c3db.json  |   8 +++
 rtemslwip/esp32c3/lwipbspopts.h | 116 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c    |  93 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 217 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 defs/bsps/riscv/esp32c3db.json
 create mode 100644 rtemslwip/esp32c3/lwipbspopts.h
 create mode 100644 rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c

diff --git a/defs/bsps/riscv/esp32c3db.json b/defs/bsps/riscv/esp32c3db.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a9860ece0853bb24ce735307fb332d9f23ec013f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/defs/bsps/riscv/esp32c3db.json
@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+{
+	"header-paths-to-import": [
+		"rtemslwip/esp32c3"
+	],
+	"source-files-to-import": [
+		"rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c"
+	]
+}
diff --git a/rtemslwip/esp32c3/lwipbspopts.h b/rtemslwip/esp32c3/lwipbspopts.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b883f4c9c638ff6e76cb61ba762003fe68293e8b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/rtemslwip/esp32c3/lwipbspopts.h
@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+/*
+ * lwIP sizing for riscv/esp32c3db.
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
+ *
+ * Every default in rtemslwip/include/lwipopts.h is #ifndef-guarded, and the
+ * defaults were chosen for parts with external DRAM.  Left alone they ask for
+ * more memory than this chip has:
+ *
+ *   MEM_SIZE          2 MiB
+ *   PBUF_POOL_SIZE    512, of PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE 1600  =  800 KiB
+ *
+ * against 384 KiB of internal SRAM, of which the BSP's RAM region is 320 KiB
+ * and about 218 KiB is free once RTEMS and the WiFi libraries have taken
+ * theirs.  That is not a tuning difference; the defaults cannot link here.
+ *
+ * The ESP32-C3 has no external RAM interface -- SOC_SPIRAM_SUPPORTED is not
+ * defined for it -- so there is no configuration in which the defaults fit.
+ * The 4 MiB of SPI flash does not help: these are all live buffers.
+ *
+ * The two largest items are sized against what they cost in .bss rather than
+ * against a guess, because on this part the whole margin is 14 KiB and a
+ * static buffer is taken from the same 272 KiB the WiFi libraries allocate
+ * from.  rtems-esphome#121 has the measurement.  Two identities are worth
+ * having in front of you before changing a number here, both confirmed
+ * against riscv-rtems7-nm on the built liblwip.a:
+ *
+ *   memp_memory_PBUF_POOL_base = PBUF_POOL_SIZE * ( sizeof( struct pbuf )
+ *                                                   + PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE )
+ *   ram_heap                   = MEM_SIZE + 2 * sizeof( struct mem )
+ *
+ * sizeof( struct pbuf ) is 16 and sizeof( struct mem ) is 6 here; MEM_ALIGNMENT
+ * is lwIP's default of 1, so neither expression carries any rounding.
+ */
+
+#ifndef RTEMSLWIP_ESP32C3_LWIPBSPOPTS_H
+#define RTEMSLWIP_ESP32C3_LWIPBSPOPTS_H
+
+/*
+ * The receive pool.  This is the largest single item and the one that decides
+ * how many frames can be in flight before the driver starts dropping.
+ *
+ * A buffer has to hold one whole frame, because rtems_esp_netif_input() asks
+ * for pbuf_alloc( PBUF_RAW, len, PBUF_POOL ) and a len larger than this is
+ * answered with a chain rather than a refusal -- which works, and quietly
+ * costs two pool entries per frame.  RTEMS_ESP_NETIF_FRAME_MAX is 1518 and
+ * the netif's MTU is 1500, so 1520 is that frame rounded up to 16.  The 1600
+ * it replaces was never a frame size; it was the rtems-lwip default.
+ *
+ * Eight of them is 12 KiB.  What has to fit at once is a full TCP receive
+ * window -- TCP_WND below is four segments -- plus whatever is queued in the
+ * tcpip mailbox behind it, so eight is about twice the depth a single
+ * station can put in flight.  The WiFi libraries have their own RX buffers in
+ * front of this -- CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_*_RX_BUFFER_NUM in the port's sdkconfig.h
+ * -- so this pool is the second queue, not the first.
+ *
+ * A pool that is too small does not report an error.  It drops frames, and
+ * the radio goes quiet under load.  lwIP counts both ends of that for us:
+ * lwip_stats.memp[ MEMP_PBUF_POOL ]->err is the refusal count and ->max the
+ * high-water mark, and MEMP_STATS is on by default.  Check them, not the
+ * absence of a complaint, before changing this number again.
+ */
+#define PBUF_POOL_SIZE     8
+#define PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE  1520
+
+/*
+ * Keep the same 8 KiB lwIP heap as the C5/S31 ports. The public network
+ * gen_server and WPA handshake also need space in C3's unified RTEMS heap.
+ * This still accommodates one TCP_SND_BUF plus small DHCP/DNS packets.
+ */
+#define MEM_SIZE           ( 8 * 1024 )
+
+/*
+ * The pools, scaled to a part that will hold a handful of connections rather
+ * than hundreds.  An ESPHome node is one API client, mDNS, and perhaps a
+ * couple of sensors reporting; MicroPython is whatever the script opens.
+ */
+#define MEMP_NUM_PBUF             16
+#define MEMP_NUM_FRAG_PBUF         8
+#define MEMP_NUM_NETCONN           8
+#define MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB           8
+#define MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN    4
+#define MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG          24
+#define MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB           6
+
+/*
+ * Four segments rather than eight in each direction: 8 * TCP_MSS is 11.6 KiB
+ * per socket per direction, which is more than this part should commit to one
+ * connection.  It costs throughput on a long fat link, which a 2.4 GHz station
+ * talking to a local access point is not.
+ */
+#define TCP_SND_BUF        ( 4 * TCP_MSS )
+#define TCP_WND            ( 4 * TCP_MSS )
+
+/*
+ * The send queue has to be set with TCP_SND_BUF, not left at its default.
+ *
+ * lwIP derives it as 16 * TCP_SND_BUF / TCP_MSS, which with the buffer above
+ * is 64 segments -- more than MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG, and lwIP's own sanity check in
+ * core/init.c refuses to build rather than let the queue outrun the pool.  It
+ * is right to: the failure would otherwise be a transmit stall under load.
+ *
+ * 4 * TCP_SND_BUF / TCP_MSS is 16, comfortably inside the 24 segments above
+ * and still four times the two lwIP documents as the floor.
+ */
+#define TCP_SND_QUEUELEN   ( ( 4 * TCP_SND_BUF ) / TCP_MSS )
+
+/*
+ * Needed by esp_wifi_internal_set_sta_ip(), which tells the WiFi libraries the
+ * station's address so they can answer ARP while the CPU sleeps.  rtems-lwip
+ * leaves it off by default; the ESP32-C3's only interface is the one that
+ * wants it.
+ */
+#define LWIP_NETIF_STATUS_CALLBACK  1
+
+#endif /* RTEMSLWIP_ESP32C3_LWIPBSPOPTS_H */
diff --git a/rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c b/rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..9df0a73fd359e9d420dc7322ba05ed876aac3c49
--- /dev/null
+++ b/rtemslwip/esp32c3/netstart.c
@@ -0,0 +1,93 @@
+/*
+ * start_networking() for riscv/esp32c3db.
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
+ *
+ * Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without
+ * modification, are permitted provided that the following conditions
+ * are met:
+ * 1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright
+ *    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.
+ * 2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright
+ *    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the
+ *    documentation and/or other materials publish with the distribution.
+ *
+ * THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS "AS IS"
+ * AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES ARE DISCLAIMED.
+ */
+
+/*
+ * Unlike every other BSP with a port here, the ESP32-C3 has no Ethernet
+ * controller.  Its only network interface is the WiFi radio, and that is
+ * driven by Espressif's binary libraries, which are not part of rtems-lwip and
+ * should not become part of it -- they are 8 closed archives and a 120-entry
+ * OS shim.
+ *
+ * So this file starts the stack and stops.  Adding the interface is left to a
+ * weak hook that the WiFi driver overrides, which keeps the dependency
+ * pointing the right way: rtems-lwip knows nothing about the WiFi port, and
+ * the WiFi port knows about lwIP.
+ *
+ * An application that links no driver still gets a working stack -- loopback,
+ * the socket API, the tcpip thread -- and a message saying why it has no
+ * interface, rather than a link error naming a symbol it has never heard of.
+ */
+
+#include <netstart.h>
+
+#include <lwip/netif.h>
+#include <lwip/tcpip.h>
+
+#include <rtems/bspIo.h>
+
+/*
+ * Overridden by the WiFi driver.  Returns 0 on success.
+ *
+ * Weak rather than a registration call because it has to run after
+ * start_networking_shared() and before start_networking() returns, and a
+ * registration API would mean either an ordering rule for the application to
+ * get wrong or a second entry point for it to forget.
+ */
+int __attribute__((weak)) esp32c3_netif_add(
+  struct netif  *net_interface,
+  ip_addr_t     *ipaddr,
+  ip_addr_t     *netmask,
+  ip_addr_t     *gateway,
+  unsigned char *mac_address
+)
+{
+  (void) net_interface;
+  (void) ipaddr;
+  (void) netmask;
+  (void) gateway;
+  (void) mac_address;
+
+  printk(
+    "esp32c3: the stack is up but there is no interface.  This part has no\n"
+    "esp32c3: Ethernet controller; link a WiFi driver providing\n"
+    "esp32c3: esp32c3_netif_add() to get one.\n"
+  );
+
+  return 1;
+}
+
+int start_networking(
+  struct netif  *net_interface,
+  ip_addr_t     *ipaddr,
+  ip_addr_t     *netmask,
+  ip_addr_t     *gateway,
+  unsigned char *mac_ethernet_address
+)
+{
+  if ( start_networking_shared() != RTEMS_SUCCESSFUL ) {
+    return 1;
+  }
+
+  return esp32c3_netif_add(
+    net_interface,
+    ipaddr,
+    netmask,
+    gateway,
+    mac_ethernet_address
+  );
+}
